--! You can create a list in beni with the `[ 1, 2, 3 ]` syntax, so lists are
--! used all over the place. This module has a bunch of functions to help you
--! work with them.
--!
--! A list is **array-backed** (`language.md` §6.8, `backend.md` §4, *Lists
--! are arrays*): reading an element by index is O(1) — O(log₃₂ n), "near
--! O(1)", on a list that has been written to — `length` is O(1), and a list
--! grows at its end in amortised O(1): `push`, `[ …xs, x ]`. Its front has
--! no room, so `[ x, …xs ]` copies the list. Each function's doc comment
--! says what it costs. A function that reads a list and builds another —
--! `map`, `filter`, `slice`, `concat`, `reverse`, the sorts, `++` — returns
--! a fresh list, never the one it was given, even when the two would be
--! equal (`backend.md` §4, *Identity*, amended 2026-10-13).
--!
--! Argument order here is **subject first and function last**: the value the
--! function is about comes first, so `xs ▷ map f` inserts it at the front,
--! and the function comes last, so a lambda you write inline is the final
--! argument. Two kinds of declaration sit outside that sentence, in opposite
--! directions. `cons` is ordered by the list syntax instead — `[ x, …xs ]`
--! is a call of it, so it is element before list rather than something you
--! pipe into. And the builders take no list at all, so "the list comes first"
--! cannot be what subject first means for them: `repeat` is still subject
--! first, with the value repeated before the count, exactly as `String.repeat`
--! is, while `range lo hi` has no subject to put first.
--!
--! The whole runtime is beni over `Js` (research 50): the views and the
--! trie — its claimable tail, and the head a prefix removal leaves — are
--! `Js.object` headers whose `$plain` is a `Js.method`, read and written
--! below the public surface.
--! Every function with a callback is a beni loop over the core-private
--! `base`, `offset` and `at`, building its result in a core-private
--! `Builder`, so a callback that suspends parks the loop (`language.md`
--! §6.8, *Callbacks*). What stays `foreign` — `length`, `at`, `put`,
--! `half` — the code generator writes in place.
--!
--! `List a` is equal to another list when they have the same elements in the
--! same order, by each element's own `eq` — which is what makes `==` on a
--! list of a type with an `eq` of its own answer that type's question and not
--! a structural walk's. It is ordered the same way, element by element by
--! each element's own `compare`, with a shorter list `LT` against a longer
--! one that starts the same.
--!
--! There is no `comparable` (`fast-compiler.md` §3.1). What replaced it is
--! the `compare` method, so the ordering functions ask for one rather than
--! for a number: `sort` for anything that has a `compare`, `sortBy` for
--! anything with a key that does, and `sortWith` for an ordering you write
--! yourself, which is how you sort by something that is not the type's own
--! order.

import Js


--| A list of values, all of the same type. `[]` is the empty list, and a
--| spread puts the elements of one list into another.
--|
--|     [ 1, 2, 3 ]
--|     [ 1, …[ 2, 3 ] ]
--|
--| Equatable when the element type is.
pub equatable sequence foreign type List a


--| What a loop of this module builds its result in: a fresh array that only
--| the loop that made it can see, written in place by `put` and handed over
--| once by `done` as a plain list (`backend.md` §4, invariant 5). Nothing
--| outside this module can name one, and each is used linearly, so the
--| write is never observable.
sequence foreign type Builder a


--| Add an element to the front of a list.
--|
--|     cons 1 [ 2, 3 ] == [ 1, 2, 3 ]
--|
--| This is what `[ x, …xs ]` desugars to, which is why the element comes
--| first here while every other function in this module takes the list first.
--|
--| O(n): a list is an array, and its front has no room, so this copies
--| `xs`. To grow a list, add at the end — `[ …xs, x ]`, `push` — which is
--| amortised O(1) and keeps the elements in the order they arrive. The one
--| O(1) case builds nothing: putting back the element a pattern just took
--| off, `[ x, …rest ]` after matching `[ x, …rest ]`, gives back the wider
--| list (`backend.md` §4, *The claimable head*, amended 2026-10-11).
pub cons : stored a, returned List a → prepended List a
cons x xs = Js.pure λ⊤ →
    t = Js.from xs
    if isTrie t && reconsed t (Js.from x) then
        Js.to
            (header
                (length xs + 1)
                (Js.get t "h")
                (Js.to (Js.get t "hc") + 1)
                (Js.get t "T")
                (Js.get t "t")
            )
    else
        b = base xs
        o = offset xs
        if o > 0 && Js.same (at b (o - 1)) x then
            if o == 1 then
                b
            else
                Js.to (viewOf (Js.from b) (o - 1))
        else if length xs < 32 then
            Js.to (pushFrom (Js.from b) o (Js.array [ Js.from x ]))
        else
            -- One native copy, several times faster than a push per element
            -- above a few dozen.
            Js.to
                (Js.call (Js.array [ Js.from x ]) "concat"
                    [ if o == 0 then
                        Js.from b
                      else
                        Js.call (Js.from b) "slice" [ Js.from o ]
                    ]
                )


--| Whether trie `a` is the tail of a trie that began with `x`: its head
--| array holds `x` in the slot past its count, so `x` then `a` is a header
--| with one more, sharing every array — a re-cons, which builds nothing.
reconsed : Js.Value, Js.Value → Bool
reconsed a x =
    hc = Js.to (Js.get a "hc")
    h = Js.get a "h"
    hc < Js.to (Js.get h "length") && Js.same (Js.at h hc) x


--| `c` with `b[i] … b[b.length - 1]` pushed onto it.
pushFrom : Js.Value, Int, Js.Value → Js.Value
pushFrom b i c =
    if i < Js.to (Js.get b "length") then
        _ = Js.call c "push" [ Js.at b i ]
        pushFrom b (i + 1) c
    else
        c


--| Put two lists together. `[ …xs, …ys ]` is this function, and `++` on
--| two lists calls it too.
--|
--|     append [ 1, 2 ] [ 3 ] == [ 1, 2, 3 ]
--|
--| When `xs` has been written to, or has 32 elements or more while `ys`
--| has fewer, each element of `ys` is pushed onto it: O(length of `ys`), so
--| `[ …xs, x ]` costs what `push xs x` does. Otherwise the result is a
--| fresh list, O(length of both) — `append xs []` and `append [] ys`
--| included, which are copies and never the list itself.
pub append : written List a, borrowed List a → concatenated List a
append xs ys = Js.pure λ⊤ →
    n = length xs
    m = length ys
    if m == 0 then
        copy xs
    else if n == 0 then
        copy ys
    else
        x = Js.from xs
        trie = isTrie x
        if (if trie then m ≥ n else n < 32 || m ≥ 32) then
            Js.to (Js.call (plainOf x) "concat" [ plainOf (Js.from ys) ])
        else
            Js.to
                (triePushed
                    (if trie then x else fromPlain (Js.from (base xs)) (offset xs))
                    (Js.from (base ys))
                    (offset ys)
                    m
                )


--| A list's elements as a plain array: itself, or its `$plain()`.
plainOf : Js.Value → Js.Value
plainOf x =
    if isPlain x then
        x
    else
        Js.call x "$plain" []


--| Determine the length of a list. O(1).
--|
--|     length [ 1, 2, 3 ] == 3
pub foreign pure length : borrowed List a → Int


--| Two lists are equal when they have the same elements, in the same order,
--| by each element's own `eq`.
--|
--|     [ 1, 2 ] == [ 1, 2 ]
--|
--| This is what `==` on a list calls. It is written here rather than derived
--| because `List a` has no constructors for the compiler to walk. O(n).
pub eq : List a, List a → Bool
    where a.eq : a, a → Bool
eq xs ys = length xs == length ys && eqFrom (base xs) (offset xs) (base ys) (offset ys)


--| Whether `a[i] …` equal `b[j] …` to the end of `a`, each by its own `eq`.
--| A list's elements end where its base array does, so the two walks end
--| together when the lists are as long.
eqFrom : List a, Int, List a, Int → Bool
    where a.eq : a, a → Bool
eqFrom a i b j =
    if i < length a then
        if at a i == at b j then
            eqFrom a (i + 1) b (j + 1)
        else
            False
    else
        True


--| Order two lists element by element, by each element's own `compare`.
--| The first pair that differs decides; if one list runs out first while
--| everything before matched, the shorter one is `LT`.
--|
--|     compare [ 1, 2 ] [ 1, 2, 3 ] == LT
--|
--| This is what `<`, `≤`, `>` and `≥` on a list call. Like `eq` it is
--| written here rather than derived, because `List a` has no constructors
--| to walk.
pub compare : List a, List a → Order
    where a.compare : a, a → Order
compare xs ys = compareFrom (base xs) (offset xs) (base ys) (offset ys)


--| The first pair of `a[i] …` and `b[j] …` that is not `EQ` decides; with
--| none, the list with elements left is `GT`, and with neither, `EQ`. Each
--| list's elements end where its base array does.
compareFrom : List a, Int, List a, Int → Order
    where a.compare : a, a → Order
compareFrom a i b j =
    if i < length a && j < length b then
        case (at a i).compare (at b j) of
            EQ →
                compareFrom a (i + 1) b (j + 1)

            o →
                o
    else if i < length a then
        GT
    else if j < length b then
        LT
    else
        EQ


--| Replace the element at an index. Out of range — a negative index, one
--| not less than the length, or one that is not a whole number — the result is the list itself, and so it is
--| when the element there is already `value` (the same value, not an equal
--| one).
--|
--|     set [ 1, 2, 3 ] 1 9 == [ 1, 9, 3 ]
--|     set [ 1, 2, 3 ] 3 9 == [ 1, 2, 3 ]
--|
--| A copy up to 256 elements; above, near O(1) after one O(n) conversion
--| per list.
pub set : written returned List a, borrowed Int, stored a → List a
set xs i v = Js.pure λ⊤ →
    n = length xs
    if outside i n then
        xs
    else if isTrie (Js.from xs) then
        Js.to (trieSet (Js.from xs) i (Js.from v))
    else
        b = base xs
        o = offset xs
        if Js.same (at b (o + i)) v then
            xs
        else if n > 256 then
            Js.to (trieSet (fromPlain (Js.from b) o) i (Js.from v))
        else
            c = Js.call (Js.from b) "slice" [ Js.from o ]
            Js.setAt c i (Js.from v)
            Js.to c


--| Add an element at the end of a list, which is where a list grows.
--|
--|     push [ 1, 2 ] 3 == [ 1, 2, 3 ]
--|
--| A copy below 32 elements; above, amortised O(1) after one O(n) conversion
--| per list. Pushing twice onto one version (after an undo) copies at most
--| 31 elements, never the list. A loop that builds a list with `push` keeps
--| the order the elements arrived in, so it needs no `reverse`.
pub push : written returned List a, stored a → List a
push xs v = Js.pure λ⊤ →
    if isTrie (Js.from xs) then
        Js.to (triePush (Js.from xs) (Js.from v))
    else
        b = base xs
        o = offset xs
        if length xs ≥ 32 then
            Js.to (triePush (fromPlain (Js.from b) o) (Js.from v))
        else
            c = Js.call (Js.from b) "slice" [ Js.from o ]
            _ = Js.call c "push" [ Js.from v ]
            Js.to c


--| Remove the last element of a list. `pop []` is the empty list itself.
--|
--|     pop [ 1, 2, 3 ] == [ 1, 2 ]
--|
--| The cost of `set`.
pub pop : written returned List a → List a
pop xs = Js.pure λ⊤ →
    n = length xs
    if n == 0 then
        xs
    else if isTrie (Js.from xs) then
        Js.to (triePop (Js.from xs))
    else
        b = base xs
        o = offset xs
        if n > 256 then
            Js.to (triePop (fromPlain (Js.from b) o))
        else
            Js.to (Js.call (Js.from b) "slice" [ Js.from o, Js.from (length b - 1) ])


--| The elements from index `from` up to but not including `to`, as Elm's
--| `Array.slice`: a negative index counts from the end, both are clamped to
--| the list, and `from` at or past `to` is `[]`. A fraction is cut toward
--| zero first, as `Array.slice` does: `slice xs 0.5 2 == slice xs 0 2`. A slice of the whole list
--| is a copy of it.
--|
--|     slice [ 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 ] 1 3 == [ 1, 2 ]
--|     slice [ 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 ] 1 (-1) == [ 1, 2, 3 ]
--|     slice [ 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 ] (-2) 5 == [ 3, 4 ]
--|
--| O(length of the result), a fresh list.
pub slice : List a, Int, Int → List a
slice xs from to =
    n = length xs
    f = clampIndex from n
    t = clampIndex to n
    if f ≥ t then
        []
    else
        sliceOf xs f t


--| An index of Elm's `Array.slice` clamped to a list of `n`: a fraction is cut
--| toward zero first, then a negative one counts from the end. NaN is 0.
clampIndex : Int, Int → Int
clampIndex i n = Js.pure λ⊤ →
    j = Js.call (Js.global "Math") "trunc" [ Js.from i ]
    if Js.to j ≥ 0 then
        Js.to (Js.call (Js.global "Math") "min" [ j, Js.from n ])
    else if Js.to j < 0 then
        Js.to (Js.call (Js.global "Math") "max" [ Js.from 0, Js.from (n + Js.to j) ])
    else
        0


--| A new list with the same elements, in the same order: the explicit copy.
--| A change to the copy is never seen through the original, nor the other
--| way round, which is what a version kept on purpose — an undo history, a
--| snapshot — wants (`docs/design/ownership-checker.md` §3.4).
--|
--|     copy [ 1, 2, 3 ] == [ 1, 2, 3 ]
--|
--| O(n), and always a fresh list, never the list itself. The elements are
--| not copied: a list of lists copies the outer list only.
pub copy : borrowed List a → copyof List a
copy xs =
    o = offset xs
    Js.pure λ⊤ → Js.to (Js.call (Js.from (base xs)) "slice" [ Js.from o, Js.from (o + length xs) ])


--| Elements `from` up to `to` of `xs`, a fresh plain array.
sliceOf : borrowed List a, borrowed Int, borrowed Int → copyof List a
sliceOf xs from to =
    o = offset xs
    Js.pure λ⊤ →
        Js.to (Js.call (Js.from (base xs)) "slice" [ Js.from (o + from), Js.from (o + to) ])


--| Insert an element before an index, so that it is at that index in the
--| result. At the length it is added at the end; out of range — negative,
--| past the length, or not a whole number — the result is the list itself.
--|
--|     insertAt [ 1, 2, 3 ] 1 9 == [ 1, 9, 2, 3 ]
--|     insertAt [ 1, 2, 3 ] 3 9 == [ 1, 2, 3, 9 ]
--|
--| O(n), a fresh list.
pub insertAt : written returned List a, borrowed Int, stored a → List a
insertAt xs i v =
    n = length xs
    if outside i (n + 1) then
        xs
    else
        b = Js.from (base xs)
        o = offset xs
        Js.pure λ⊤ →
            Js.to
                (Js.call (Js.call b "slice" [ Js.from o, Js.from (o + i) ]) "concat"
                    [ Js.array [ Js.from v ]
                    , Js.call b "slice" [ Js.from (o + i), Js.from (o + n) ]
                    ]
                )


--| Remove the element at an index. Out of range — as for `set` — the result
--| is the list itself.
--|
--|     removeAt [ 1, 2, 3 ] 1 == [ 1, 3 ]
--|
--| O(n), a fresh list.
pub removeAt : written returned List a, borrowed Int → List a
removeAt xs i =
    n = length xs
    if outside i n then
        xs
    else
        b = Js.from (base xs)
        o = offset xs
        Js.pure λ⊤ →
            Js.to
                (Js.call (Js.call b "slice" [ Js.from o, Js.from (o + i) ]) "concat"
                    [ Js.call b "slice" [ Js.from (o + i + 1), Js.from (o + n) ] ]
                )


--| Exchange the elements at two indexes. When either is out of range (as
--| for `set`), or they are the same index, the result is the list itself.
--|
--|     swap [ 1, 2, 3 ] 0 2 == [ 3, 2, 1 ]
--|
--| Two `set`s.
pub swap : written returned List a, borrowed Int, borrowed Int → List a
swap xs i j =
    n = length xs
    if outside i n || outside j n || i == j then
        xs
    else
        x = unsafeGet xs i
        y = unsafeGet xs j
        set (set xs i y) j x


--| Element `i`, for `0 ≤ i < length xs`, which the caller guarantees: out
--| of range it would read `undefined` as a value of any type, which is why
--| it is not `pub` (`backend.md` §4). The code generator imports it for a
--| list pattern's elements.
unsafeGet : borrowed List a, borrowed Int → elementof a
unsafeGet xs i = Js.pure λ⊤ →
    v = Js.from xs
    if isPlain v then
        Js.to (Js.at v i)
    else if Js.isUndefined (Js.get v "o") then
        Js.to (trieGet v i)
    else
        Js.to (Js.at (Js.get v "b") (Js.to (Js.get v "o") + i))


-- Whether `i` is no index below `n`: negative, not less than `n`, a fraction
-- or NaN (an `Int` is a double, and `2 ^ (0 - 1)` is `0.5`). `i >>> 0` is `i`
-- exactly when `i` is a whole number from 0 to 4294967295, which holds every
-- length, so one test covers each of those.
outside : Int, Int → Bool
outside i n = Js.shiftRightZero i 0 ≠ i || i ≥ n


--| The list without its first `k` elements, `0 ≤ k ≤ length`: the list
--| itself at 0, `[]` at the end, O(1) otherwise. What a pattern's
--| `[ x, …rest ]` binds `rest` to; the code generator imports it.
view : returned List a, borrowed Int → List a
view xs k = Js.pure λ⊤ →
    if k == 0 then
        xs
    else if k ≥ length xs then
        []
    else if isTrie (Js.from xs) then
        Js.to (trieDrop (Js.from xs) k)
    else
        Js.to (viewOf (Js.from (base xs)) (offset xs + k))


--| The plain array a list is a suffix of, and where in it the list starts
--| (`backend.md` §8, *Scalar views*): the list itself and 0, a view's
--| backing array and offset, a trie's plain copy (made once) and 0. Every
--| loop below reads its list through these two and `at`.
base : returned List a → List a
base xs = Js.pure λ⊤ →
    v = Js.from xs
    if isPlain v then
        xs
    else if Js.isUndefined (Js.get v "o") then
        Js.to (Js.call v "$plain" [])
    else
        Js.to (Js.get v "b")


offset : borrowed List a → Int
offset xs = Js.pure λ⊤ →
    v = Js.from xs
    if isPlain v || Js.isUndefined (Js.get v "o") then
        0
    else
        Js.to (Js.get v "o")


--| Element `i` of an array `base` returned, `i` below its length: `a[i]`.
--| The code generator writes it in place (`backend.md` §4, *`List.beni`'s
--| loops read and write in place*), so every loop has a property access of
--| its own, which stays monomorphic where one shared function's goes
--| megamorphic across a program's loops.
foreign pure at : borrowed List a, borrowed Int → elementof a


--| The traversal primitive (`ownership-checker.md` §18.5): element `i` of an
--| array `base` returned, read as `at` reads it and written in place the
--| same way, `a[i]`. What it adds is its ownership row, which is asserted:
--| the element it hands out is separate from every other position of the
--| array, so a loop may hand it to a callback that edits it in place when
--| the list is unique, exclusive and dead after the loop. The obligation it
--| carries, which every loop below meets: the body that calls it reads `a`
--| only through it, once per index, at an index that moves one way.
foreign pure traverseAt : borrowed List a, borrowed Int → taken a


--| A builder with room for `n` elements: a loop that knows how many it will
--| write fills them by index with `put`, which builds in a third of the
--| time pushing takes at 100 000 elements, where an array grown by `push`
--| is copied as it grows.
builder : unused Int → fresh exclusive Builder a
builder n = Js.pure λ⊤ →
    Js.to (Js.construct (Js.global "Array") [ Js.from (if n > 0 then n else 0) ])


--| Element `i` of the builder written, `i` below the room it was made with.
--| Written in place, as `at` is: `b[i] = x`.
foreign pure put : written returned Builder a, borrowed Int, stored a → Builder a


--| The builder handed over as a list of its first `n` elements: `n` is
--| how many a loop `put`, so a builder made with more room
--| than it needed — `filter`'s — is cut to what it holds.
done : written returned Builder a, borrowed Int → builderdone List a
done b n = Js.pure λ⊤ →
    v = Js.from b
    if Js.to (Js.get v "length") == n then
        ⊤
    else
        Js.set v "length" (Js.from n)
    Js.to v


--| A building loop's exit (`backend.md` §8, *Tail calls modulo cons, onto
--| an array*): the loop's own destination with `rest`'s elements pushed
--| after it, or `rest` itself when the destination is empty. The code
--| generator writes the calls, and the destination is the loop's alone
--| until this hands it over, which is what makes the push sound.
close : List a, List a → List a
close b v =
    n = length b
    if n == 0 then
        v
    else
        done (putAll (Js.to (Js.from b)) n (base v) (offset v) (offset v + length v)) (n + length v)


--| `n // 2` for a non-negative `n`, without `Basics`' sibling.
foreign pure half : Int → Int


-- ---- The three forms, over `Js` (`backend.md` §4, *Lists are arrays*) ----
--
-- A view is `{ b, o, length, p, $plain }` and a trie `{ length, h, hc, T,
-- t, p, $plain }`, each made by one `Js.object` so every one has one shape;
-- the tree is `{ r, s, off, tc }`. Their fields are read by name, which no
-- build renames.

--| A view's `$plain()`: its elements as a fresh plain array, made once.
viewPlain : Js.Value
viewPlain = Js.method λv →
    cached = Js.get v "p"
    if Js.isNull cached then
        copied = Js.call (Js.get v "b") "slice" [ Js.get v "o" ]
        Js.set v "p" copied
        copied
    else
        cached


--| A trie's `$plain()`: its plain copy, made once per header — the head
--| backwards, each leaf, then the tail, into an array made its size.
triePlain : Js.Value
triePlain = Js.method λa →
    cached = Js.get a "p"
    if Js.isNull cached then
        n = Js.to (Js.get a "length")
        tr = Js.get a "T"
        off = Js.to (Js.get tr "off")
        b = Js.construct (Js.global "Array") [ Js.from n ]
        j = flatHead (Js.get a "h") (Js.to (Js.get a "hc") - 1) b 0
        k = flatTree (Js.get tr "r") (Js.to (Js.get tr "s")) off (off + Js.to (Js.get tr "tc")) b j
        _ = copyTo (Js.get a "t") 0 b k n
        Js.set a "p" b
        b
    else
        cached


flatHead : Js.Value, Int, Js.Value, Int → Int
flatHead h i b j =
    if i ≥ 0 then
        Js.setAt b j (Js.at h i)
        flatHead h (i - 1) b (j + 1)
    else
        j


flatTree : Js.Value, Int, Int, Int, Js.Value, Int → Int
flatTree r s i stop b j =
    if i < stop then
        flatTree r s (i + 32) stop b (copyTo (leafAt r s i) 0 b j (j + 32))
    else
        j


--| `x[i] …` copied into `b[j] … b[stop - 1]`; `stop`. A leaf's 32 and the
--| tail's rest, one walk.
copyTo : Js.Value, Int, Js.Value, Int, Int → Int
copyTo x i b j stop =
    if j < stop then
        Js.setAt b j (Js.at x i)
        copyTo x (i + 1) b (j + 1) stop
    else
        j


isPlain : Js.Value → Bool
isPlain = Js.to (Js.get (Js.global "Array") "isArray")


isTrie : Js.Value → Bool
isTrie v = not (Js.isUndefined (Js.get v "T"))


isView : Js.Value → Bool
isView v = not (Js.isUndefined (Js.get v "o"))


--| The suffix of plain array `b` from `o`, `1 ≤ o < b.length`.
viewOf : Js.Value, Int → Js.Value
viewOf b o =
    Js.object
        [ ( "b", b )
        , ( "o", o )
        , ( "length", Js.to (Js.get b "length") - o )
        , ( "p", Js.null )
        , ( "$plain", viewPlain )
        ]


header : Int, Js.Value, Int, Js.Value, Js.Value → Js.Value
header n h hc tr t =
    Js.object
        [ ( "length", n )
        , ( "h", h )
        , ( "hc", hc )
        , ( "T", tr )
        , ( "t", t )
        , ( "p", Js.null )
        , ( "$plain", triePlain )
        ]


treeOf : Js.Value, Int, Int, Int → Js.Value
treeOf r s off tc = Js.object [ ( "r", r ), ( "s", s ), ( "off", off ), ( "tc", tc ) ]


noTree : Js.Value
noTree = Js.pure λ⊤ → Js.object [ ( "r", Js.array [] ), ( "s", 5 ), ( "off", 0 ), ( "tc", 0 ) ]


--| The leaf holding radix position `i` of a root `r` at shift `s`.
leafAt : Js.Value, Int, Int → Js.Value
leafAt r s i =
    if s > 0 then
        leafAt (Js.at r (Js.bitAnd (Js.shiftRightZero i s) 31)) (s - 5) i
    else
        r


--| Element `i` of trie `a`.
trieGet : Js.Value, Int → Js.Value
trieGet a i =
    hc = Js.to (Js.get a "hc")
    if i < hc then
        Js.at (Js.get a "h") (hc - 1 - i)
    else
        tr = Js.get a "T"
        j = i - hc
        tc = Js.to (Js.get tr "tc")
        if j ≥ tc then
            Js.at (Js.get a "t") (j - tc)
        else
            k = j + Js.to (Js.get tr "off")
            Js.at (leafAt (Js.get tr "r") (Js.to (Js.get tr "s")) k) (Js.bitAnd k 31)


--| Trie `a` without its first `k` elements, `0 < k < length`.
trieDrop : Js.Value, Int → Js.Value
trieDrop a k =
    len = Js.to (Js.get a "length")
    hc = Js.to (Js.get a "hc")
    if k ≤ hc then
        header (len - k) (Js.get a "h") (hc - k) (Js.get a "T") (Js.get a "t")
    else
        tr = Js.get a "T"
        k2 = k - hc
        tc = Js.to (Js.get tr "tc")
        if k2 ≥ tc then
            Js.call (Js.get a "t") "slice" [ Js.from (k2 - tc), Js.from (len - hc - tc) ]
        else
            r = Js.get tr "r"
            s = Js.to (Js.get tr "s")
            i = Js.to (Js.get tr "off") + Js.bitAnd k2 (-32)
            front =
                Js.call (Js.call (leafAt r s i) "slice" [ Js.from (Js.bitAnd k2 31) ]) "reverse" []
            header
                (len - k)
                front
                (32 - Js.bitAnd k2 31)
                (treeOf r s (i + 32) (tc - Js.bitAnd k2 (-32) - 32))
                (Js.get a "t")


--| A path copy: node `x` (null: a new node) with `leaf` at radix position
--| `i`; a gap left of a new child is filled with null.
putLeaf : Js.Value, Int, Int, Js.Value → Js.Value
putLeaf x s i leaf =
    c =
        if Js.isNull x then
            Js.array []
        else
            Js.call x "slice" []
    j = Js.bitAnd (Js.shiftRightZero i s) 31
    padNull c j
    Js.setAt c j
        (if s == 5 then
            leaf
         else
            putLeaf
                (if j < Js.to (Js.get c "length") then
                    Js.at c j
                 else
                    Js.null
                )
                (s - 5)
                i
                leaf
        )
    c


padNull : Js.Value, Int → ⊤
padNull c j =
    if Js.to (Js.get c "length") < j then
        _ = Js.call c "push" [ Js.null ]
        padNull c j


--| A copy of node `x` without the leaf at radix position `i` and
--| everything right of it; null when nothing is left.
popLeaf : Js.Value, Int, Int → Js.Value
popLeaf x s i =
    j = Js.bitAnd (Js.shiftRightZero i s) 31
    if s == 5 then
        prefixOrNull x j
    else
        c = popLeaf (Js.at x j) (s - 5) i
        if Js.isNull c then
            prefixOrNull x j
        else
            y = Js.call x "slice" []
            Js.setAt y j c
            y


prefixOrNull : Js.Value, Int → Js.Value
prefixOrNull x j =
    if j == 0 then
        Js.null
    else
        Js.call x "slice" [ Js.from 0, Js.from j ]


--| Tree `tr` with `leaf` added at its right end.
treeRight : Js.Value, Js.Value → Js.Value
treeRight tr leaf =
    tc = Js.to (Js.get tr "tc")
    if tc == 0 then
        rightGrow (Js.array []) 5 0 0 leaf
    else
        rightGrow (Js.get tr "r") (Js.to (Js.get tr "s")) (Js.to (Js.get tr "off")) tc leaf


rightGrow : Js.Value, Int, Int, Int, Js.Value → Js.Value
rightGrow r s off tc leaf =
    if off + tc ≥ 2 ^ (s + 5) then
        rightGrow (Js.array [ r ]) (s + 5) off tc leaf
    else
        treeOf (putLeaf r s (off + tc) leaf) s off (tc + 32)


--| THE CLAIM: buffer `b` of a version that reads its first `c` elements,
--| with `v` at index `c` — in place when this version owns `b`'s end,
--| shared when `b` already holds `v` there, a copy otherwise.
claim : Js.Value, Int, Js.Value → Js.Value
claim b c v =
    if Js.to (Js.get b "length") == c then
        _ = Js.call b "push" [ v ]
        b
    else if Js.same (Js.at b c) v then
        b
    else
        copied = Js.call b "slice" [ Js.from 0, Js.from c ]
        _ = Js.call copied "push" [ v ]
        copied


--| Buffer `b`'s first `c` elements with index `k` set to `v`: a copy.
changed : Js.Value, Int, Int, Js.Value → Js.Value
changed b c k v =
    copied = Js.call b "slice" [ Js.from 0, Js.from c ]
    Js.setAt copied k v
    copied


--| Plain array `b` from index `o` as a trie: an empty head, the tree at
--| offset 0, the last 1 to 32 elements the tail, built level by level.
fromPlain : Js.Value, Int → Js.Value
fromPlain b o =
    n = Js.to (Js.get b "length") - o
    full = Js.shiftLeft (Js.shiftRightZero (n - 1) 5) 5
    if full == 0 then
        header n (Js.array []) 0 noTree (Js.call b "slice" [ Js.from o ])
    else
        fromLevels b o n full (chunks b o (o + full) (Js.array [])) 5


fromLevels : Js.Value, Int, Int, Int, Js.Value, Int → Js.Value
fromLevels b o n full x s =
    if Js.to (Js.get x "length") > 32 then
        fromLevels b o n full (chunks x 0 (Js.to (Js.get x "length")) (Js.array [])) (s + 5)
    else
        header n (Js.array []) 0 (treeOf x s 0 full) (Js.call b "slice" [ Js.from (o + full) ])


--| `out` with `x[i … i + 31]`, `x[i + 32 …]`, … up to `stop` pushed onto
--| it, 32 to an array: the leaves of a plain array, and each level above
--| them, written once.
chunks : Js.Value, Int, Int, Js.Value → Js.Value
chunks x i stop out =
    if i < stop then
        _ = Js.call out "push" [ Js.call x "slice" [ Js.from i, Js.from (i + 32) ] ]
        chunks x (i + 32) stop out
    else
        out


--| Trie `a` with element `i` replaced: a copy of the head, the tail or the
--| leaf it falls in and the path to it; `a` itself when it is already `v`.
trieSet : Js.Value, Int, Js.Value → Js.Value
trieSet a i v =
    if Js.same (trieGet a i) v then
        a
    else
        n = Js.to (Js.get a "length")
        hc = Js.to (Js.get a "hc")
        tr = Js.get a "T"
        if i < hc then
            header n (changed (Js.get a "h") hc (hc - 1 - i) v) hc tr (Js.get a "t")
        else
            j = i - hc
            tc = Js.to (Js.get tr "tc")
            if j ≥ tc then
                header n (Js.get a "h") hc tr (changed (Js.get a "t") (n - hc - tc) (j - tc) v)
            else
                k = j + Js.to (Js.get tr "off")
                r = Js.get tr "r"
                s = Js.to (Js.get tr "s")
                header
                    n
                    (Js.get a "h")
                    hc
                    (treeOf
                        (putLeaf r s k (changed (leafAt r s k) 32 (Js.bitAnd k 31) v))
                        s
                        (Js.to (Js.get tr "off"))
                        tc
                    )
                    (Js.get a "t")


--| Trie `a` with `v` at the end: the tail claimed; a full tail moves into
--| the tree as a leaf.
triePush : Js.Value, Js.Value → Js.Value
triePush a v =
    n = Js.to (Js.get a "length")
    hc = Js.to (Js.get a "hc")
    tr = Js.get a "T"
    c = n - hc - Js.to (Js.get tr "tc")
    if c < 32 then
        header (n + 1) (Js.get a "h") hc tr (claim (Js.get a "t") c v)
    else
        header (n + 1) (Js.get a "h") hc (treeRight tr (Js.get a "t")) (Js.array [ v ])


--| Trie `a` with `b[o] … b[o + m - 1]` at the end, `m > 0`, one header.
--| They are the last elements of `b` (a list is a suffix of its base), so
--| a slice of 32 from `i` stops where they do.
triePushed : Js.Value, Js.Value, Int, Int → Js.Value
triePushed a b o m =
    n = Js.to (Js.get a "length")
    hc = Js.to (Js.get a "hc")
    tr = Js.get a "T"
    c = n - hc - Js.to (Js.get tr "tc")
    if c < 32 then
        t0 = Js.get a "t"
        t =
            if Js.to (Js.get t0 "length") == c then
                t0
            else
                Js.call t0 "slice" [ Js.from 0, Js.from c ]
        i = fillTail t b o (o + m)
        pushedLeaves a (n + m) tr t b i (o + m)
    else
        pushedLeaves a (n + m) tr (Js.get a "t") b o (o + m)


fillTail : Js.Value, Js.Value, Int, Int → Int
fillTail t b i stop =
    if Js.to (Js.get t "length") < 32 && i < stop then
        _ = Js.call t "push" [ Js.at b i ]
        fillTail t b (i + 1) stop
    else
        i


pushedLeaves : Js.Value, Int, Js.Value, Js.Value, Js.Value, Int, Int → Js.Value
pushedLeaves a n tr t b i stop =
    if i < stop then
        pushedLeaves
            a
            n
            (treeRight tr t)
            (Js.call b "slice" [ Js.from i, Js.from (i + 32) ])
            b
            (i + 32)
            stop
    else
        header n (Js.get a "h") (Js.to (Js.get a "hc")) tr t


--| Trie `a` without its last element.
triePop : Js.Value → Js.Value
triePop a =
    n = Js.to (Js.get a "length")
    if n ≤ 1 then
        Js.array []
    else
        hc = Js.to (Js.get a "hc")
        tr = Js.get a "T"
        tc = Js.to (Js.get tr "tc")
        if n - hc - tc > 1 then
            header (n - 1) (Js.get a "h") hc tr (Js.get a "t")
        else if tc == 0 then
            Js.call (Js.call (Js.get a "h") "slice" [ Js.from 0, Js.from hc ]) "reverse" []
        else
            off = Js.to (Js.get tr "off")
            r = Js.get tr "r"
            s = Js.to (Js.get tr "s")
            lastLeaf = off + tc - 32
            lf = leafAt r s lastLeaf
            if lastLeaf == off then
                header (n - 1) (Js.get a "h") hc noTree lf
            else
                popped = popLeaf r s lastLeaf
                collapse
                    (if Js.isNull popped then
                        Js.array []
                     else
                        popped
                    )
                    s
                    off
                    (lastLeaf - 1)
                    (n - 1)
                    (Js.get a "h")
                    hc
                    lf
                    (tc - 32)


--| A root left with one live child collapses into it; `n`, `h`, `hc` and
--| `t` are the rest of the result.
collapse : Js.Value, Int, Int, Int, Int, Js.Value, Int, Js.Value, Int → Js.Value
collapse r s off end n h hc t tc =
    if s > 5 && Js.shiftRightZero off s == Js.shiftRightZero end s then
        j = Js.shiftRightZero off s
        collapse (Js.at r j) (s - 5) (off - j * 2 ^ s) end n h hc t tc
    else
        header n h hc (treeOf r s off tc) t


--| Reduce a list from the left, starting from an initial value.
--|
--|     foldl [ 1, 2, 3 ] [] cons == [ 3, 2, 1 ]
--|
--| `func` sees the first element first. The loop is a tail call, which the
--| code generator turns into a loop (`backend.md` §8), so this walks a list
--| of any length.
pub foldl : List a, b, (a, b → b) → b
foldl xs acc func = foldlFrom (base xs) (offset xs) (offset xs + length xs) acc func


--| Fold `a[i]`, `a[i + 1]`, … `a[stop - 1]` of an array `base` returned.
foldlFrom : List a, Int, Int, b, (a, b → b) → b
foldlFrom a i stop acc func =
    if i < stop then
        foldlFrom a (i + 1) stop (func (traverseAt a i) acc) func
    else
        acc


--| Reduce a list from the right, starting from an initial value.
--|
--|     foldr [ 1, 2, 3 ] [] cons == [ 1, 2, 3 ]
--|
--| This is the one function here that calls its callback back to front, and
--| that is its contract rather than an accident of how it is written
--| (`checker.md` Appendix B): the last element is handed to `func` first.
--| Nothing built on it may inherit that order — see `map`. It walks the
--| list backwards by index and allocates nothing.
pub foldr : List a, b, (a, b → b) → b
foldr xs acc func = foldrFrom (base xs) (offset xs) (offset xs + length xs - 1) acc func


foldrFrom : List a, Int, Int, b, (a, b → b) → b
foldrFrom a o i acc func =
    if i < o then
        acc
    else
        foldrFrom a o (i - 1) (func (traverseAt a i) acc) func


--| Create a list with only one element.
--|
--|     singleton 1234 == [ 1234 ]
pub singleton : a → List a
singleton value = [ value ]


--| Create a list with `n` copies of a value.
--|
--|     repeat 0 3 == [ 0, 0, 0 ]
pub repeat : a, Int → List a
repeat value n = repeatHelp value 0 n (builder n)


repeatHelp : a, Int, Int, Builder a → List a
repeatHelp value i n b =
    if i < n then
        repeatHelp value (i + 1) n (put b i value)
    else
        done b i


--| Create a list of numbers, every element increasing by one. You give the
--| lowest and the highest number that should be in the list.
--|
--|     range 3 6 == [ 3, 4, 5, 6 ]
--|     range 3 3 == [ 3 ]
--|     range 6 3 == []
pub range : Int, Int → List Int
range lo hi = rangeHelp lo 0 (hi - lo + 1) (builder (hi - lo + 1))


rangeHelp : Int, Int, Int, Builder Int → List Int
rangeHelp lo i n b =
    if i < n then
        rangeHelp lo (i + 1) n (put b i (lo + i))
    else
        done b i


--| Create a list of `n` elements, calling `func` on each index from 0 to
--| `n - 1` in that order. A count of zero or less is `[]`.
--|
--|     initialize 4 (λi → i * i) == [ 0, 1, 4, 9 ]
--|
--| O(n).
pub initialize : Int, (Int → a) → List a
initialize n func = initializeHelp 0 n func (builder n)


initializeHelp : Int, Int, (Int → a), Builder a → List a
initializeHelp i n func b =
    if i < n then
        initializeHelp (i + 1) n func (put b i (func i))
    else
        done b i


--| Apply a function to every element of a list.
--|
--|     map [ 1, 4, 9 ] negate == [ -1, -4, -9 ]
--|
--| `func` is called on the first element first (`checker.md` Appendix B).
--| The result is a fresh list, even when every result is the element it
--| was given (`backend.md` §4, *Identity*). O(n), and the result reads at
--| O(1).
pub map : List a, (a → b) → List b
map xs func = mapHelp (base xs) (offset xs) 0 (length xs) func (builder (length xs))


--| Every loop below reads element `i` of its list as `at a (o + i)`, `a`
--| and `o` being the list's `base` and `offset`; a loop that hands the
--| element to a callback reads it with `traverseAt`.
mapHelp : List a, Int, Int, Int, (a → b), Builder b → List b
mapHelp a o i n func b =
    if i < n then
        mapHelp a o (i + 1) n func (put b i (func (traverseAt a (o + i))))
    else
        done b n


--| Same as `map` but the function is also given the index of each element,
--| starting at zero. The result is a fresh list, as `map`'s is.
--|
--|     indexedMap [ "a", "b" ] (λi x → ( i, x )) == [ ( 0, "a" ), ( 1, "b" ) ]
pub indexedMap : List a, (Int, a → b) → List b
indexedMap xs func = indexedMapHelp (base xs) (offset xs) 0 (length xs) func (builder (length xs))


indexedMapHelp : List a, Int, Int, Int, (Int, a → b), Builder b → List b
indexedMapHelp a o i n func b =
    if i < n then
        indexedMapHelp a o (i + 1) n func (put b i (func i (traverseAt a (o + i))))
    else
        done b n


--| Keep the elements that pass the test.
--|
--|     filter [ 1, 2, 3, 4 ] (λn → Int.mod n 2 == 0) == [ 2, 4 ]
--|
--| `isGood` is asked about the first element first. The result is a fresh
--| list, even when it keeps every element (`backend.md` §4, *Identity*).
--| O(n).
pub filter : List a, (a → Bool) → List a
filter xs isGood = filterHelp (base xs) (offset xs) 0 (length xs) isGood (builder (length xs)) 0


--| `j` is how many were kept: the builder has room for all that are left.
filterHelp : List a, Int, Int, Int, (a → Bool), Builder a, Int → List a
filterHelp a o i n isGood b j =
    if i < n then
        x = traverseAt a (o + i)
        if isGood x then
            filterHelp a o (i + 1) n isGood (put b j x) (j + 1)
        else
            filterHelp a o (i + 1) n isGood b j
    else
        done b j


--| Filter out certain values. For example, to get all the heads of a list of
--| lists, filter out the empty ones in the same pass that unwraps the rest.
--|
--|     filterMap [ [ 1, 2 ], [], [ 3 ] ] head == [ 1, 3 ]
--|
--| `func` sees the first element first. O(n).
pub filterMap : List a, (a → Maybe b) → List b
filterMap xs func = filterMapHelp (base xs) (offset xs) 0 (length xs) func (builder (length xs)) 0


filterMapHelp : List a, Int, Int, Int, (a → Maybe b), Builder b, Int → List b
filterMapHelp a o i n func b j =
    if i < n then
        case func (traverseAt a (o + i)) of
            Just y →
                filterMapHelp a o (i + 1) n func (put b j y) (j + 1)

            Nothing →
                filterMapHelp a o (i + 1) n func b j
    else
        done b j


--| Reverse a list.
--|
--|     reverse [ 1, 2, 3 ] == [ 3, 2, 1 ]
--|
--| A fresh list, even of fewer than two elements (`backend.md` §4,
--| *Identity*). O(n).
pub reverse : List a → List a
reverse xs = reverseHelp (base xs) (offset xs + length xs - 1) 0 (length xs) (builder (length xs))


--| `top` is the index in `a` of the list's last element.
reverseHelp : List a, Int, Int, Int, Builder a → List a
reverseHelp a top i n b =
    if i < n then
        reverseHelp a top (i + 1) n (put b i (traverseAt a (top - i)))
    else
        done b n


--| Figure out whether a list contains a value. The element type has to have
--| an `eq`, which rules out lists of functions.
--|
--|     member [ 1, 2, 3 ] 9 == False
--|     member [ 1, 4, 9 ] 4 == True
pub member : List a, a → Bool
    where a.eq : a, a → Bool
member xs value = any xs λx → x == value


--| Determine if all elements satisfy some test.
--|
--|     all [ 2, 4 ] (λn → Int.mod n 2 == 0) == True
--|     all [] (λn → Int.mod n 2 == 0) == True
pub all : List a, (a → Bool) → Bool
all xs isOkay = not (any xs λx → not (isOkay x))


--| Determine if any elements satisfy some test.
--|
--|     any [ 2, 3 ] (λn → Int.mod n 2 == 0) == True
--|     any [] (λn → Int.mod n 2 == 0) == False
pub any : List a, (a → Bool) → Bool
any xs isOkay = anyFrom (base xs) (offset xs) (offset xs + length xs) isOkay


anyFrom : List a, Int, Int, (a → Bool) → Bool
anyFrom a i stop isOkay =
    if i ≥ stop then
        False
    else if isOkay (at a i) then
        True
    else
        anyFrom a (i + 1) stop isOkay


--| Find the maximum element in a non-empty list of numbers. Elm's version
--| takes any `comparable`; this one takes numbers, and folding with `max`
--| over a key function covers the rest.
--|
--|     maximum [ 1, 4, 2 ] == Just 4
--|     maximum [] == Nothing
pub maximum : List number → Maybe number
maximum xs =
    if isEmpty xs then
        Nothing
    else
        Just (foldlFrom (base xs) (offset xs + 1) (offset xs + length xs) (unsafeGet xs 0) max)


--| Find the minimum element in a non-empty list of numbers.
--|
--|     minimum [ 3, 2, 1 ] == Just 1
--|     minimum [] == Nothing
pub minimum : List number → Maybe number
minimum xs =
    if isEmpty xs then
        Nothing
    else
        Just (foldlFrom (base xs) (offset xs + 1) (offset xs + length xs) (unsafeGet xs 0) min)


--| Get the sum of the list elements.
--|
--|     sum [ 1, 2, 3 ] == 6
pub sum : List number → number
sum xs = foldl xs 0 (+)


--| Get the product of the list elements.
--|
--|     product [ 2, 2, 2 ] == 8
pub product : List number → number
product xs = foldl xs 1 (*)


--| `a[i]` … `a[stop - 1]`, put into builder `b` from index `j` on.
putAll : Builder a, Int, List a, Int, Int → Builder a
putAll b j a i stop =
    if i < stop then
        putAll (put b j (at a i)) (j + 1) a (i + 1) stop
    else
        b


--| Concatenate a bunch of lists into a single list.
--|
--|     concat [ [ 1, 2 ], [ 3 ], [] ] == [ 1, 2, 3 ]
--|
--| A fresh list made its final size, even when only one of the lists is
--| not empty (`backend.md` §4, *Identity*), O(total): one count, then one
--| copy loop per list.
pub concat : List (List a) → List a
concat ls =
    a = base ls
    o = offset ls
    n = totalFrom a o 0
    done (concatFrom a o (builder n) 0) n


--| How many elements the lists `a[i] …` to the end of `a` hold together. A
--| list's elements end where its base array does, so every loop here stops
--| at `length a`.
totalFrom : List (List a), Int, Int → Int
totalFrom a i n =
    if i < length a then
        totalFrom a (i + 1) (n + length (at a i))
    else
        n


--| The lists `a[i] …` written into `b` from `j` on.
concatFrom : List (List a), Int, Builder a, Int → Builder a
concatFrom a i b j =
    if i < length a then
        l = at a i
        concatFrom a (i + 1) (putAll b j (base l) (offset l) (offset l + length l)) (j + length l)
    else
        b


--| Map a given function onto a list and flatten the resulting lists.
--|
--|     concatMap [ 1, 2 ] (λx → [ x, x ]) == [ 1, 1, 2, 2 ]
--|
--| `func` is called on the first element first, and once per element. It
--| is `concat (map xs func)`, a fresh list even when exactly one of the
--| answers is not empty. The answers are kept in a list of
--| their own first, so the result is made its final size once, which is
--| faster than growing it as the answers come.
pub concatMap : List a, (a → List b) → List b
concatMap xs func = concat (map xs func)


--| Places the given value between all members of the given list.
--|
--|     intersperse [ "turtles", "turtles" ] "on" == [ "turtles", "on", "turtles" ]
pub intersperse : List a, a → List a
intersperse xs sep =
    if isEmpty xs then
        []
    else
        intersperseHelp
            (base xs)
            (offset xs)
            sep
            1
            (length xs)
            (put (builder (2 * length xs - 1)) 0 (unsafeGet xs 0))


intersperseHelp : List a, Int, a, Int, Int, Builder a → List a
intersperseHelp a o sep i n b =
    if i < n then
        intersperseHelp a o sep (i + 1) n (put (put b (2 * i - 1) sep) (2 * i) (at a (o + i)))
    else
        done b (2 * n - 1)


--| Combine two lists, combining them with the given function. If one list is
--| longer, the extra elements are dropped.
--|
--|     map2 [ 1, 2, 3 ] [ 10, 20 ] (+) == [ 11, 22 ]
--|
--| `func` is called on the first elements first, and not at all for the tail
--| the shorter list does not reach. `map3`–`map5` are the same, one list
--| wider each.
pub map2 : List a, List b, (a, b → result) → List result
map2 xs ys func =
    n = min (length xs) (length ys)
    map2Help (base xs) (offset xs) (base ys) (offset ys) 0 n func (builder n)


--| `ax` and `ox` are `xs`'s base and offset, `ay` and `oy` `ys`'s, and so on
--| for `map3`–`map5`: each is a loop of its own, so that its call of `func`
--| is too.
map2Help : List a, Int, List b, Int, Int, Int, (a, b → result), Builder result → List result
map2Help ax ox ay oy i n func b =
    if i < n then
        map2Help ax ox ay oy (i + 1) n func (put b i (func (at ax (ox + i)) (at ay (oy + i))))
    else
        done b n


--| Combine three lists, combining them with the given function.
pub map3 : List a, List b, List c, (a, b, c → result) → List result
map3 xs ys zs func =
    n = min (length xs) (min (length ys) (length zs))
    map3Help (base xs) (offset xs) (base ys) (offset ys) (base zs) (offset zs) 0 n func (builder n)


map3Help :
      List a
    , Int
    , List b
    , Int
    , List c
    , Int
    , Int
    , Int
    , (a, b, c → result)
    , Builder result
    → List result
map3Help ax ox ay oy az oz i n func b =
    if i < n then
        map3Help
            ax
            ox
            ay
            oy
            az
            oz
            (i + 1)
            n
            func
            (put b i (func (at ax (ox + i)) (at ay (oy + i)) (at az (oz + i))))
    else
        done b n


--| Combine four lists, combining them with the given function.
pub map4 : List a, List b, List c, List d, (a, b, c, d → result) → List result
map4 xs ys zs ws func =
    n = min (min (length xs) (length ys)) (min (length zs) (length ws))
    map4Help
        (base xs)
        (offset xs)
        (base ys)
        (offset ys)
        (base zs)
        (offset zs)
        (base ws)
        (offset ws)
        0
        n
        func
        (builder n)


map4Help :
      List a
    , Int
    , List b
    , Int
    , List c
    , Int
    , List d
    , Int
    , Int
    , Int
    , (a, b, c, d → result)
    , Builder result
    → List result
map4Help ax ox ay oy az oz aw ow i n func b =
    if i < n then
        map4Help
            ax
            ox
            ay
            oy
            az
            oz
            aw
            ow
            (i + 1)
            n
            func
            (put b i (func (at ax (ox + i)) (at ay (oy + i)) (at az (oz + i)) (at aw (ow + i))))
    else
        done b n


--| Combine five lists, combining them with the given function.
pub map5 :
      List a
    , List b
    , List c
    , List d
    , List e
    , (a, b, c, d, e → result)
    → List result
map5 xs ys zs ws vs func =
    n = min (min (length xs) (length ys)) (min (min (length zs) (length ws)) (length vs))
    map5Help
        (base xs)
        (offset xs)
        (base ys)
        (offset ys)
        (base zs)
        (offset zs)
        (base ws)
        (offset ws)
        (base vs)
        (offset vs)
        0
        n
        func
        (builder n)


map5Help :
      List a
    , Int
    , List b
    , Int
    , List c
    , Int
    , List d
    , Int
    , List e
    , Int
    , Int
    , Int
    , (a, b, c, d, e → result)
    , Builder result
    → List result
map5Help ax ox ay oy az oz aw ow av ov i n func b =
    if i < n then
        map5Help
            ax
            ox
            ay
            oy
            az
            oz
            aw
            ow
            av
            ov
            (i + 1)
            n
            func
            (put
                b
                i
                (func
                    (at ax (ox + i))
                    (at ay (oy + i))
                    (at az (oz + i))
                    (at aw (ow + i))
                    (at av (ov + i))
                )
            )
    else
        done b n


--| Sort a list from lowest to highest, by the element type's own `compare`.
--|
--|     sort [ 3, 1, 5 ] == [ 1, 3, 5 ]
--|
--| Elm sorts any `comparable`; beni has none, so this one asks the element
--| type for its `compare` method and sorts by what it answers.
pub sort : List a → List a
    where a.compare : a, a → Order
sort xs = sortWith xs λx y → x.compare y


--| Sort values by a key read out of each of them. The key can be of any
--| type that has a `compare`, not only a number.
--|
--|     sortBy [ { age = 30 }, { age = 20 } ] .age == [ { age = 20 }, { age = 30 } ]
--|
--| `toKey` is called **exactly once per element, on the first element
--| first** (`checker.md` Appendix B), which is what decorate–sort–undecorate
--| buys: `map` reads the keys in list order, the pairs are sorted by their
--| first component alone, and `map` reads the elements back out. The
--| spelling this replaced, `sortWith xs (λx y → (toKey x).compare (toKey
--| y))`, called `toKey` once per COMPARISON — six times for a three-element
--| list, and not at all for a one-element one — so an expensive key was
--| recomputed and an effectful one would run its effect a number of times
--| nobody could predict. The COMPARATOR's order and count stay unspecified,
--| which is inherent to a sort.
--|
--| Ties are `EQ` on the key, and the merge takes the left element on `EQ`,
--| so this is as stable as `sortWith` is. The result is a fresh list, even
--| of fewer than two elements (`backend.md` §4, *Identity*).
pub sortBy : List a, (a → b) → List a
    where b.compare : b, b → Order
sortBy xs toKey =
    decorated = map xs λx → ( toKey x, x )
    map (sortWith decorated λ( left, _ ) ( right, _ ) → left.compare right) λ( _, x ) → x


--| Sort values with an ordering function you write yourself, which is how
--| you sort by something that is not the type's own order.
--|
--|     sortWith [ 1, 2, 3 ] (λx y → Basics.compare y x) == [ 3, 2, 1 ]
--|
--| The sort is a stable merge sort: equal elements keep the order they were
--| given in, and that is a contract rather than an accident of the merge
--| (`checker.md` Appendix B) — the merge takes the LEFT element on anything
--| but `GT`, and the front half, the first ⌊n / 2⌋ elements, stays in front.
--| The order in which `cmp` is asked, and how many times, stays unspecified.
--| The result is a fresh list, even of fewer than two elements
--| (`backend.md` §4, *Identity*). O(n log n), recursing to a depth of
--| log₂ n.
pub sortWith : borrowed List a, many (a, a → Order) → copyof List a
sortWith xs cmp = sortRange xs 0 (length xs) cmp


--| Elements `lo` up to `hi` of `xs`, sorted: the front half first, then the
--| back half, then the two merged.
sortRange : List a, Int, Int, (a, a → Order) → List a
sortRange xs lo hi cmp =
    if hi - lo < 2 then
        slice xs lo hi
    else
        mid = lo + half (hi - lo)
        left = sortRange xs lo mid cmp
        right = sortRange xs mid hi cmp
        merge left 0 (length left) right 0 (length right) cmp (builder (hi - lo))


--| Two sorted lists merged. On anything but `GT` the left element wins,
--| which is what makes `sortWith` stable; what is left of one list when the
--| other runs out is added after. Both are plain arrays — `sortRange`'s
--| `slice` of at most one element of a longer list, or a `merge` — so they
--| are read by `at` with no `base`.
merge : List a, Int, Int, List a, Int, Int, (a, a → Order), Builder a → List a
merge xs i n ys j m cmp b =
    if i ≥ n then
        done (putAll b (i + j) ys j m) (n + m)
    else if j ≥ m then
        done (putAll b (i + j) xs i n) (n + m)
    else
        x = at xs i
        y = at ys j
        case cmp x y of
            GT →
                merge xs i n ys (j + 1) m cmp (put b (i + j) y)

            _ →
                merge xs (i + 1) n ys j m cmp (put b (i + j) x)


--| Determine if a list is empty.
--|
--|     isEmpty [] == True
pub isEmpty : List a → Bool
isEmpty xs = length xs == 0


--| Extract the first element of a list.
--|
--|     head [ 1, 2, 3 ] == Just 1
--|     head [] == Nothing
pub head : List a → Maybe a
head xs = get xs 0


--| Extract the rest of the list.
--|
--|     tail [ 1, 2, 3 ] == Just [ 2, 3 ]
--|     tail [] == Nothing
--|
--| O(1): the rest is a view of the list, sharing its elements, which keeps
--| the whole list alive as a substring may. Of a list that has been written
--| to it is the same list without its first element, sharing its structure.
pub tail : List a → Maybe (List a)
tail xs =
    if isEmpty xs then
        Nothing
    else
        Just (view xs 1)


--| Take the first `n` members of a list. A fraction is cut toward zero.
--|
--|     take [ 1, 2, 3 ] 2 == [ 1, 2 ]
--|
--| O(n), a fresh list even when `n` covers the whole list (`backend.md` §4,
--| *Identity*).
pub take : List a, Int → List a
take xs n =
    if n ≤ 0 then
        []
    else
        slice xs 0 n


--| Drop the first `n` members of a list.
--|
--|     drop [ 1, 2, 3 ] 2 == [ 3 ]
--|
--| O(1), as `tail` is, and `drop xs 0` is `xs` itself. A fraction is cut toward
--| zero: `drop xs 1.5 == drop xs 1`.
pub drop : List a, Int → List a
drop xs n =
    if n ≤ 0 then
        xs
    else
        view xs (clampIndex n (length xs))


--| The element at an index, counting from 0, or `Nothing` when the index is
--| negative, not less than the length, or not a whole number.
--|
--|     get [ 10, 20, 30 ] 1 == Just 20
--|     get [ 10, 20, 30 ] 3 == Nothing
--|
--| Near O(1).
pub get : List a, Int → Maybe a
get xs i =
    if outside i (length xs) then
        Nothing
    else
        Just (unsafeGet xs i)


--| Extract the last element of a list.
--|
--|     last [ 1, 2, 3 ] == Just 3
--|     last [] == Nothing
--|
--| Near O(1).
pub last : List a → Maybe a
last xs = get xs (length xs - 1)


--| Replace the element at an index with what `func` makes of it. `func` is
--| called once in range and not at all out of range, where the result is
--| the list itself.
--|
--|     update [ 1, 2, 3 ] 1 (λx → x * 10) == [ 1, 20, 3 ]
--|
--| The cost of `set`, and its identities.
pub update : List a, Int, (a → a) → List a
update xs i func = focus xs i func


--| The focus primitive (`ownership-checker.md` §18.5): element `i` taken out
--| of the list, handed to `func`, and the result put back where it was. Its
--| ownership row is asserted: the element is handed out while no other
--| position holds it, so `func` may edit it in place when the list is
--| unique, exclusive and dead after the call. `update` is this.
focus : written returned List a, borrowed Int, once taken stored (a → a) → List a
focus xs i func =
    if outside i (length xs) then
        xs
    else
        set xs i (func (unsafeGet xs i))


--| Partition a list based on some test. The first list contains all values
--| that satisfy the test, and the second list contains all the value that do
--| not.
--|
--|     partition [ 1, 2, 3, 4 ] (λn → Int.mod n 2 == 0) == ( [ 2, 4 ], [ 1, 3 ] )
--|
--| `pred` is asked about each element once, the first element first. O(n).
pub partition : List a, (a → Bool) → List a × List a
partition xs pred =
    partitionHelp
        (base xs)
        (offset xs)
        0
        (length xs)
        pred
        (builder (length xs))
        (builder (length xs))
        0


--| `k` is how many passed: the rest, `i - k`, did not.
partitionHelp : List a, Int, Int, Int, (a → Bool), Builder a, Builder a, Int → List a × List a
partitionHelp a o i n pred yes no k =
    if i < n then
        x = at a (o + i)
        if pred x then
            partitionHelp a o (i + 1) n pred (put yes k x) no (k + 1)
        else
            partitionHelp a o (i + 1) n pred yes (put no (i - k) x) k
    else
        ( done yes k, done no (n - k) )


--| Decompose a list of tuples into a tuple of lists.
--|
--|     unzip [ ( 0, True ), ( 17, False ) ] == ( [ 0, 17 ], [ True, False ] )
pub unzip : List (a × b) → List a × List b
unzip pairs =
    unzipHelp
        (base pairs)
        (offset pairs)
        0
        (length pairs)
        (builder (length pairs))
        (builder (length pairs))


unzipHelp : List (a × b), Int, Int, Int, Builder a, Builder b → List a × List b
unzipHelp a o i n xs ys =
    if i < n then
        case at a (o + i) of
            ( x, y ) →
                unzipHelp a o (i + 1) n (put xs i x) (put ys i y)
    else
        ( done xs n, done ys n )
